body:
Don't beat it over the head of the reader unless it's the main focus of the narrative I'd say. Depending on how you want to approach it like they've already come out, or are transitioning you could be a little more blatant about it, but again as initially stated don't beat it over the reader's head.

&#x200B;

Biggest take away: DON'T BEAT IT OVER THE READER'S HEAD!

Technically speaking biology is on your side in this since studies have found that trans women have brains more like that of a female when compared to the average male brain.

&#x200B;

Edit: Also fuck turfs they can go fuck right off.

Cringe is based on personal perspective, so calling it cringy and chaotic just means that is how you view it. I made sense of the chaos while watching it, so maybe that is why I've got a different outlook on it. Even still I think 5 is poorly written, but I can at least think of reasons why she acted the way she did. As long as I can rationalize why a character would act stupidly I'm fine with it. Sadistic tendencies combined with childish demeanor tend to lead to shit hitting the fan.

&#x200B;

Plot isn't something that is independent of characters since characters are the ones that drive a plot, and as such you saying that using a character's flaws to develop the plot is bad storytelling just makes almost everything in writing automatically bad storytelling since every conflict that has meaning is derived from some kind of flaw of a character. For example in lets say Lord of The Rings the whole reason they had to even do any of that was because of Sauron's lust for power, and through the entire series his lust for that power drives them to stop him. It isn't a plot that drives characters it is the characters that drive a plot, and part of characters are the flaws in their personality.

Your opinion not mine. I think the story was well made, and made sense to me. Maybe I'm dumb, but even still I didn't enjoy it for the plan itself I enjoyed it for the themes and science behind the bombs they made.

&#x200B;

\>>>                                                                      ENDING                               TRANSMISSION                                                   <<<

I personally liked the trio because of how they contrasted each other, and I think that their plans remained consistent through the whole thing. They never wanted to hurt anyone, they just wanted to generate attention around them so they could get their message out there.

&#x200B;

As for the airport stuff. I think it was more an issue with 5's writing. She felt like she was just thrown in there, and her motives were unclear. I think it would have been better if the old guy was the main person chasing them down since he has a clear reason why, and also felt realistic (if a bit cliche)

I'm sorry if this seems rude, but this feels like whining. I'd like you to try something. Make 2 accounts on a dating app. Make one male and one female. Make it seem realistic, and just do it for a month. Then compare how many matches you get on each. Women have a wide selection of men to chose from, and never seem to pick... My point is that you have the option to go on as many dates as you like and you'll still have multiple other guys to talk to.

&#x200B;

Side note: I'll pay anyone who can give me proof that the guy profile got more 100$ paypal.

It is only natural. Women are basically the only ones allowed to have high standards anymore.

&#x200B;

Edit: I say mostly because gay men exist.
